            "creation_time": "2026-11-06T19:02:45Z",
            "text": "readChar() on a raw vector, in a UTF-8 locale whose MB_CUR_MAX is 4 (macOS, and it seems Windows), can write past the end of its output buffer.\n\nReproducible example: attached as readchar-utf8-buffer-repro.R; it should print \"ok\", but R 4.6.1 on macOS seg.faults in the gc() that follows readChar(). An AddressSanitizer build reports a heap-buffer-overflow WRITE in rawFixedString().\n\nAnalysis: rawFixedString() in src/main/connections.c sizes its buffer as R_MB_CUR_MAX * nchars + 1 but copies utf8clen() bytes per character, which is 6 for lead bytes 0xfc to 0xff. With more than 4 * nchars + 1 bytes left in the vector, it copies up to 6 * nchars input bytes. readFixedString() (the connection path) has the same sizing but overruns by at most a byte. glibc's MB_CUR_MAX is 6, so Linux is not affected.\n\nProposed patch: attached as readchar-utf8-buffer.diff, against r90844. It sizes both buffers by 6, the longest length utf8clen() returns, and adds a test to tests/reg-tests-1e.R; results are unchanged where nothing overflowed. I found no CRAN or Bioconductor package that calls readChar() on a raw vector in a way that reaches the overflow.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/456, where CI results can be seen.",
